<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Functions - Compositions and Inverses</title>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>A,B</m>, and <m>C</m> be sets, <m>g: A\to B</m> and
                <m>f:B\to C</m>.
                Then the <term>composition</term> function is denoted
                <m>f\circ g</m> and is defined as thus:
                <md>
                    (f\circ g): A\to C \hspace{.2in} \text{    where    }  \hspace{.2in} (f\circ g)(a) = f(g(a)).
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                This is read as <q> <m>f</m> composed with <m>g</m></q> or
                <q>
                <m>f</m> of <m>g</m> </q>.
                Note <m>g</m> composed with <m>f</m> is usually very different
                than <m>f</m> composed with <m>g</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                For a set <m>A</m>, the <term>identity function</term> on
                <m>A</m> is the function.
                That is,
                <md>
                    i_A: A\to A   \,\,\text{    where    } \,\, i_A(x) = x \text{ for every } x\in A.
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The <term>inverse</term> of a function <m>f: A\to B</m>, if it
                exists, is the function <m>f^{-1}: B\to A</m> such that
                <m>f^{-1}\circ f = i_A</m> and <m>f\circ f^{-1} = i_B</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <activity>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                For each of the following, draw arrow diagrams for <m>f</m>,
                <m>g</m>, and <m>f\circ g</m>.
                Then determine if each of those 3 functions is an injection or
                surjection.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose <m>g: \{1,2,3\} \to \{a,b,c,d\}</m> and
                    <m>f: \{a,b,c,d\} \to \{\star, \bigcirc, \bigtriangleup\}</m>
                    are defined by
                    <md>
                        g(1) = a; \,\,\, g(2) = b; \,\,\, g(3) = a \hspace{.2in} \text{ and } \hspace{.2in} f(a) = \bigcirc; \,\,\, f(b) = \bigcirc;\,\,\, f(c) = \star; \,\,\, f(d) = \bigtriangleup;
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose <m>g: \{1,2,3\} \to \{a,b,c,d\}</m> and
                    <m>f: \{a,b,c,d\} \to \{\star, \bigcirc, \bigtriangleup\}</m>
                    are defined by
                    <md>
                        g(1) = a; \,\,\, g(2) = b; \,\,\, g(3) = c \hspace{.2in} \text{ and } \hspace{.2in} f(a) = \bigcirc; \,\,\, f(b) = \star;\,\,\, f(c) = \bigtriangleup; \,\,\, f(d) = \bigtriangleup;
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity workspace="1in">
        <title>Conjectures About Compositions and 'Jections</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                We will spend some time thinking about compositions effect on
                injections and surjections.
                In each, assume <m>g: A\to B</m> and <m>f: B\to C</m>, so that
                the composition makes sense.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Must the composition of two injections be an injection?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Must the composition of two surjections be a surjection?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>f\circ g</m> is an injection, must both <m>f</m> and
                    <m>g</m> be injections?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>f\circ g</m> is a surjection, must both <m>f</m> and
                    <m>g</m> be surjections?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Theorems About Compositions and 'Jections</title>

        <p>
            The composition of two injections is always an injection, and the
            composition of two surjections is always a surjection.
            There are proof sketches and formal proofs in your book (pages 349 -
            350).
            We'll state and prove that the composition of injections is always
            an injection.
            here.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <theorem xml:id="ch08-thm-compinj">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>A,B,</m> and <m>C</m> are sets, <m>g:A \to B</m> is
                injective and <m>f:B\to C</m> is injective.
                Then <m>f\circ g</m> is injective.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proof>
        <p>
            We'll prove this directly.
            When trying to figure out a direct proof, you should always write
            down what you are assuming, what you are proving, and then
            definitions of each of those.
            Here I've outlined the order I would think abou this proof.
        </p>

