            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p workspace=".5in">
                        FIRST: Assuming (by hypothesis): <m>f</m> is
                        <fillin characters="20"/> This means for all
                        <m>b_1, b_2 \in B</m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p workspace=".5in">
                        SECOND: Assuming (by hypothesis): <m>g</m> is
                        <fillin characters="20"/> This means for all
                        <m>b_1, b_2 \in B</m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        FOURTH: The conclusion definition is a conditional
                        statement, so we will assume its hypothesis which is
                        <fillin characters="20"/> which can be rewritten as
                        <fillin characters="20"/>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p workspace="2in">
                        FIFTH: Work your way to the conclusion.
                        Hint in footnote!
                        <fn>Hint - you know <m>f(g(a_1))= f(g(a_2))</m>, what can you say about <m>g(a_1)</m> and <m>g(a_2)</m> given that <m>f</m> is an injection. </fn>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p workspace=".5in">
                        THIRD: Proving (conclusion): <fillin characters="30"/>
                        This means:
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </proof>

    <p>
        Here's a fill in the blank proof of <xref ref="ch08-thm-compinj"/>
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="ch08-thm-compisinj">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>A,B,</m> and <m>C</m> are sets, <m>g:A \to B</m> is
                injective and <m>f:B\to C</m> is injective.
                Then <m>f\circ g</m> is injective.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proof>
        <p workspace=".5in">
            Suppose <m>A,B,</m> and <m>C</m> are sets and that <m>f: B\to C</m>
            is injective.
            We will show <m>f\circ g</m> is injective by showing that
        </p>

        <p workspace=".5in">
            To this end, assume <m>a_1,a_2\in A</m> and
            <m>(f \circ g)(a_1) = (f\circ g)(a_2)</m>.
            We aim to show: <fillin characters="20"/> Applying the definition of
            composition to <m>(f \circ g)(a_1) = (f\circ g)(a_2)</m> we get
        </p>

        <p>
            Since <m>f: B\to C</m> is <fillin characters="20"/>, if
            <fillin characters="20"/> for any <m>b_1, b_2 \in B</m>, then
            <fillin characters="20"/>.
            In particular, observe that <m>g(a_1), g(a_2)\in B</m> and
            <m>f(g(a_1))=f(g(a_2))</m> and so <fillin characters="20"/>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Likewise, <m>g: A\to B</m> is <fillin characters="20"/> and we just
            showed that <m>g(a_1)=g(a_2)</m> where <m>a_1, a_2\in A</m>.
            This implies <fillin characters="20"/>.
        </p>

        <p>
            We have shown that for <m>a_1,a_2 \in A</m>, if
            <m>(f\circ g)(a_1) = (f\circ g)(a_2)</m> then <m>a_1 = a_2</m>.
            Thus, <m>f\circ g</m> is an injection.
        </p>
    </proof>

  

    <activity workspace="4in">
        <task>
            <p>
                Do a proof sketch that the composition of two surjections is a
                surjection. 
            </p>
        </task>

        <task>
            <p>
                Proof sketch: (Start by writing down what you are assuming at
                the top and what you are proving at the bottom, then write
                definitions of each.)
            </p>
        </task>
