    <p>
        Here's a fill in the blank proof that the composition of two surjections
        has to be a surjection.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="ch08-thm-compissurj">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>A,B,</m> and <m>C</m> are sets, <m>g:A \to B</m> is
                surjective and <m>f:B\to C</m> is surjective.
                Then <m>f\circ g</m> is surjective.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proof>
        <p workspace=".5in">
            We assume that <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m> are subsets of some
            universal set.
            Furthermore, we are given that function <m>f: A \to B</m> is a
            surjection.
            By definition, this means that: 
            
        </p>

        <p workspace=".5in">
            Also, we are given that <m>g: B \to C</m> is a surjection.
            By definition, this means that: 
        </p>

        <p>
            We want to show that <m>g \circ f : A \to C</m> is also a
            surjection.
            That is, we must show that for all
            <m> <fillin characters="5" /> \in <fillin characters="5" /></m>,
            there exists <m> c \in <fillin characters="5" /> </m> such that <m> <fillin characters="5" /> = <fillin characters="5" /> </m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            We begin. by assuming that <m>c \in C</m>.
            Because <m>g</m> is a surjection with codomain <m>C</m>, and
            <m>c \in C</m>, we know that there exists
            <m> <fillin characters="5" /> \in <fillin characters="5" /></m>, such
            that  <m> <fillin characters="10" />.</m>
        </p>

        <p workspace="1in">
            Because <m>f</m> is a surjection with codomain <m>B</m>, we know
        </p>

        <p>
            Putting these facts together, we have: <md>
              (g \circ
            f)( <fillin characters="5" /> ) = g(f( <fillin characters="5" /> ))  = <fillin characters="5" />
            </md>

        </p>

        <p workspace=".5in">
            Therefore, we have shown that: 
        </p>
    </proof>

    <corollary>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>A,B,</m> and <m>C</m> are sets, <m>g:A \to B</m> is
                bijective and <m>f:B\to C</m> is bijective.
                Then <m>f\circ g</m> is bijective.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </corollary>

    <proof>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are bijections. Then <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are both injections and surjections. So by <xref ref="ch08-thm-compisinj"/> and <xref ref="ch08-thm-compissurj"/>, <m>f\circ g</m> is an injection and a surjection.
      </p>
    </proof>

    <p>
        It is also true that if <m>f\circ g</m> is an injection then <m>g</m> is
        an injection, and if <m>f\circ g</m> is a surjection then <m>f</m> is a
        surjection.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="ch08-thm-compsurjfsurj">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>A,B,</m> and <m>C</m> are sets, and <m>g: A\to B</m>
                and <m>f: B\to C</m> are functions.
                If <m>f\circ g</m> is surjective, then <m>f</m> is surjective.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

