        What are you proving?
        Write that at the bottom.
        Write definitions of each!
    </p>

</activity>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Inverse Functions</title>

        <p/>
    </paragraphs>

    <theorem>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A function <m>f: A\to B</m> is invertible if and only if
                <m>f</m> is a bijection.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
        There's a proof of this theorem on pages 355-356 of your textbook, which
        I encourage you to read.
        These 2 pictures from your preview activity reading show the big idea
        behind why if <m>f</m> is not a bijection, then <m>f^{-1}</m> is not a
        function.
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="40% 40%">
        <figure>
            <caption>
                A function that is not an injection.
                Why does it not have an inverse?
            </caption>

            <image source="images/InverseNotInj.png">
                <shortdescription>
                    An arrow diagram showing a non-injective function.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <figure>
            <caption>
                A function that is not a surjection.
                Why does it not have an inverse?
            </caption>

            <image source="images/InverseNotSurj.png">
                <shortdescription>
                    An arrow diagram showing a non-surjective function.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </sidebyside>

    <p workspace="1in"/>
    <activity workspace="1in">
        <p>
            In college algebra, you were likely asked many times to find the
            inverse of a function like <m>f(x)=x^3-5</m>.
            Work with your group to try to remember ways you were taught to do
            that.
            Then think about how that relates to the definition given in your
            text.
        </p>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                One of the big ideas of inverse functions is that they allow us
                to <q>undo</q> functions.
                E.g., <m>\log_2(x)</m> undoes <m>2^x</m> in the sense that
                <m>\log_2(2^x) = x = 2^{\log_2(x)}</m>.
                Similarly, <m>\arcsin(x)</m> undoes <m>\sin(x)</m>.
                This activity is going to ask, if we can take square roots and
                cube roots modulo <m>5</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Define <m>f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}</m> by
                    <m>f(x) = x^2</m>.
                    Then we might say the inverse is
                    <m>g: \mathbb{R} \to\mathbb{R}</m> where
                    <m>g(x) = \sqrt{x}</m>.
                    Is <m>g</m> a function?
                    Why or why not?
                    What about if we change <m>f</m> to <m>x^3</m> and <m>g</m>
                    to <m>\sqrt[3]{x}</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>R_5 = \{0,1,2,3,4\}</m> for the rest of the
                    questions.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Define <m>h: R_5 \to R_5</m> by <m>h(x)=x^2 \pmod{5}</m>.
                    Find <m>h(a)</m> for all <m>a\in R_5</m>.
                    Does <m>h</m> have an inverse function?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Define <m>j: R_5 \to R_5</m> by <m>j(x)=x^3 \pmod{5}</m>.
                    Find <m>j(a)</m> for all <m>a\in R_5</m>.
                    Does <m>j</m> have an inverse function?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Define <m>k: R_5 \to R_5</m> by <m>k(x)=x^3 -2 \pmod{5}</m>.
                    Can you find a formula for <m>k^{-1}(x)</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <p>
        In the following activities, start by doing some examples of where a few
        elements in the domain map to in the codomain.
        Then <q> push</q> on it a little bit - try to get elements of the domain
        to map to the same element in the codomain.
        Try to find an element that doesn't get mapped to, and do some scratch
        work to see if it does.
        For functions that are from <q> one dimension</q> to
        <q> one
                dimension</q> you might graph (I would use
        Desmos).
    </p>

    <activity>
        <title>More Practice With 'Jections</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Prove that each of the following are both injections and
                surjections (and hence bijections).
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <p>
                <m>g: \mathbb{R}-\{1\} \to \mathbb{R}-\{1\}</m> defined by
                <m>g(x) = \frac{x}{x-1}</m>.
            </p>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <p>
                <m>f: \mathbb{R} \to S</m> defined by
                <m>f(x) = 2\cdot 3^{4x-1} + 7</m>, where
                <m>S = \{y\in\mathbb{R}: y&gt;7\}</m>.
            </p>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <p>
                <m>h: \mathbb{R}\times \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}</m>
                defined by <m>h(x,y) = (-x+y, 2y)</m>.
            </p>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity workspace="2in">
        <title>Conjecturing, Proving, and Disproving</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                For each of the following, prove or disprove whether they are
                injections and/or surjections.
                See the advice before the previous activity!
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <p>
                Define
                <m>f: \mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}\times \mathbb{R}</m>
                by <m>f(a,b) = (2a+b,2a-b)</m>.
            </p>
        </task>

        <task>
            <p>
                Define <m>f: \mathbb{Z} \to\mathbb{Z}</m> by <m>f(x) = 2x-4</m>.
            </p>
        </task>

        <task>
            <p>
                Define
                <m>f: \mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}\times \mathbb{R}</m>
                by <m>f(a,b) = (2a+b,2a-b)</m>.
            </p>
        </task>

        <task>
            <p>
                Define <m>f: \mathbb{Z} \to\mathbb{Z}</m> by <m>f(x) = 2x-4</m>.
            </p>
        </task>
    </activity>
</handout>